    "When the numerators match, the question changes. Three fifths and three sevenths both have three pieces, but fifths are bigger pieces than sevenths.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-d014d97e4d928334.mp3" },
    "That is the part students often reverse: with the same numerator, the smaller denominator gives the larger pieces.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-8a00e31a0fe6fd87.mp3" },
    "Try a same-numerator comparison and picture the piece sizes if you need to.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-513a191ecd1bdcdc.mp3" },
    "So there are two clean rules: same denominator, compare how many pieces; same numerator, compare how big the pieces are.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-0d363224fdba2aa9.mp3" },
