    "Look at this half. I'm going to cut every piece into smaller pieces - but I'm not changing how much is shaded.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-2bc4b4a5c6cb65ab.mp3" },
    "Each half is splitting into two equal pieces. Same bar. Same shaded amount. Just more, smaller pieces.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-4b801a376e5518eb.mp3" },
    "How many of the four smaller pieces are shaded now?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-ab9579adbaec87ef.mp3" },
    "Here is the name for what you have just seen: one half and two quarters are equivalent fractions. Different numbers, same amount.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-dc2cebc181308601.mp3" },
    "Now watch three fifths. If every fifth is split into three equal pieces, the denominator becomes fifteen - and the shaded pieces triple as well.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-34163cb341616fe3.mp3" },
    "The denominator was multiplied by three. What has to happen to the numerator?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-10210817cbbfaf30.mp3" },
    "That's the rule. Whatever multiplier you use on the bottom, use the same multiplier on the top. The numbers change; the value doesn't.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-0a9c9eb26364fd09.mp3" },
    "One quick check. Two fifths becomes four sevenths. Equivalent or not?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-2332fc01364220d8.mp3" },
    "Now compare the valid pair: two fifths and four tenths. The pieces are smaller, but the shaded amount still ends at the same point.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-100778104f515b87.mp3" },
    "You have seen the idea from the picture and from the numbers. I will stay with you for one question, then the support starts to come away.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-d7af8e53b579fb7b.mp3" },
    "I'll give you the scale factor this time. Five has become fifteen - that's times three. What should happen to the two?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-0cc1d9a36b8d11ff.mp3" },
