    "Last check. Two questions, no hints while you're answering. This is just to see whether the idea holds when I step away.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-f780c35a414ade6f.mp3" },
    "That was a really strong finish. You have learned the key idea and you were able to use it on your own: equivalent fractions can look different while the amount stays the same. You can call it there for today, or keep going while it is fresh.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-dd01c71c59caf016.mp3" },
    "You have made good progress here. The scale-factor idea is not quite automatic yet, so I would rather give it one more short pass than rush you into the next skill. We will keep the next round focused and make it click.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-ebdbc524f881d8ed.mp3" },
    "Not applicable in diagnostic.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-d97e3bc95b2a6ace.mp3" },
    "Two of the four smaller pieces are shaded, so one half and two quarters show the same amount.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-81976e6d2b4fbcf1.mp3" },
    "Look only at the shaded half. How many smaller pieces is that shaded part split into now?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-3ccd34afc6931263.mp3" },
    "Watch the shaded half split once more. Nothing new is being shaded - we're only cutting it into smaller pieces.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-f0fbff992a057211.mp3" },
    "The shaded half becomes 2 of the 4 equal pieces. So one half equals two quarters.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-0e3a3f53e5125f11.mp3" },
    "Count only the shaded smaller pieces.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-40acdddb154988b1.mp3" },
    "The original shaded half was split into two pieces.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-ff7922a58173e17b.mp3" },
    "The shaded half becomes 2 of the 4 smaller pieces, so 1/2 = 2/4.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-36462082f272dad3.mp3" },
    "The denominator used times three, so the numerator uses times three as well.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-52a2de5820302410.mp3" },
    "Compare the three on top with the nine. What multiplication takes three to nine?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-be480619745d0da4.mp3" },
    "Three times three is nine. Now notice the bottom: five times three is fifteen. Same multiplier on both.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-b243c8a2f56d667a.mp3" },
    "Both numerator and denominator are multiplied by 3, so 3/5 becomes 9/15.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-fe6536dd7683bbaf.mp3" },
    "Compare 3 with 9.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-2d79506a6ce7865a.mp3" },
    "3 × ? = 9.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-3f07edc1bb270bcb.mp3" },
    "3 × 3 = 9 and 5 × 3 = 15, so both parts use the same multiplier, 3.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-227519bfdb9338c4.mp3" },
    "If the top doubles, the bottom must double too. Five would become ten, not seven.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-456d6edae9f7b6b0.mp3" },
    "The numerator changed from two to four. That's times two. What would five become if it also used times two?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-35dd9259af68027e.mp3" },
    "Five times two is ten, not seven. So four sevenths cannot be equivalent to two fifths.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-d9261095249e64bd.mp3" },
    "Doubling 2/5 gives 4/10, not 4/7.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-4ff360b66f781aa5.mp3" },
    "The numerator doubled. What would happen to 5 if the denominator used the same multiplier?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-3e068105d89576d0.mp3" },
    "5 × 2 = 10, not 7.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-5b920c8aff20a4fb.mp3" },
    "For equivalence, both numerator and denominator must be multiplied by the same factor. Doubling 2/5 gives 4/10, not 4/7.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-7ae1dabac7392417.mp3" },
    "Five to fifteen is times three, so two to six has to use times three as well.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-6c1c711df1d86510.mp3" },
    "The denominator has already told you the multiplier: five to fifteen is times three. Do the same thing to two.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-f907688af9cd93d6.mp3" },
    "Two times three is six. Watch the two shaded fifths split into three smaller pieces each.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-cb4f9a3b66fba397.mp3" },
    "2 x 3 = 6 and 5 x 3 = 15, so 2/5 = 6/15.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-6363796aad4e903f.mp3" },
    "5 became 15 by multiplying by 3. Do the same to 2.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-85b8047db9074ea0.mp3" },
    "2 × 3 = ?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-b09557be3cff2db6.mp3" },
    "2 × 3 = 6 and 5 × 3 = 15, so 2/5 = 6/15.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-cc6511d599789d81.mp3" },
    "Four to twelve is times three, so seven to twenty-one follows the same scale factor.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-897baba8706558ed.mp3" },
    "Start with the side you can compare. What multiplier changes four into twelve?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-2cfad0bbe99c213e.mp3" },
    "That's times three. Mirror that multiplier on the denominator: seven times three is twenty-one.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-15cb8e5e429f0480.mp3" },
    "4 x 3 = 12, so 7 x 3 = 21. Therefore 4/7 = 12/21.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-072c0525d441ae76.mp3" },
    "What multiplier changes 4 into 12?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-88b9d2e108f08f88.mp3" },
    "Use that same multiplier on 7.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-726c71d40dd7fa3b.mp3" },
    "4 × 3 = 12, so 7 × 3 = 21. Therefore 4/7 = 12/21.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-387b968a2ba073dc.mp3" },
    "You found the multiplier from six to twenty-four and then used that same multiplier on five.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-8f2a19755d4f3d5e.mp3" },
    "Compare six with twenty-four. Find that multiplier first, then use it on five.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-4cfc9f95b9e6af69.mp3" },
    "Six times four is twenty-four, so five also needs times four. Five times four is twenty.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-43f81392f5b28054.mp3" },
    "5 x 4 = 20 and 6 x 4 = 24, so 5/6 = 20/24.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-2ed8405ca9f891f8.mp3" },
    "Compare 6 and 24.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-0114ae21385de926.mp3" },
    "6 × 4 = 24, so use ×4 on the numerator too.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-d01128ea240dafa8.mp3" },
    "5 × 4 = 20 and 6 × 4 = 24, so 5/6 = 20/24.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-e11b82a34c607f36.mp3" },
    "Both numbers doubled, so three eighths became six sixteenths without changing value.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-cc656e3d87ecc337.mp3" },
    "Multiply both parts by two - not just one of them.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-a454324aad3ecd6d.mp3" },
    "Three times two is six, and eight times two is sixteen.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-231652de31da991f.mp3" },
