    "(3 × 2)/(8 × 2) = 6/16.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-6f87931238ab04ef.mp3" },
    "Five to fifteen is times three, so nine to twenty-seven uses times three as well.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-a713664be22c6fcd.mp3" },
    "Read the multiplier from five to fifteen first.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-a83f82a39dd9b583.mp3" },
    "Five times three is fifteen. Now nine times three gives twenty-seven.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-fbf6b6d54005fa50.mp3" },
    "5 x 3 = 15 and 9 x 3 = 27, so 5/9 = 15/27.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-fa2acc757fc603a5.mp3" },
    "5 became 15. What is the scale factor?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-9e84c476c58c9337.mp3" },
    "Use ×3 on 9.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-02a98d54dc65f9ea.mp3" },
    "5 × 3 = 15, so 9 × 3 = 27. The missing denominator is 27.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-6bdc88a70bb8a2d5.mp3" },
    "Doubling both seven and ten gives fourteen twentieths.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-c1466ef70989165d.mp3" },
    "Check both numbers in each option. One matching number isn't enough.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-4121176de5e8b3ce.mp3" },
