    "Look for an option where both 7 and 10 were changed by the same multiplier.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-aa73dbaea8d59404.mp3" },
    "Doubling 7 gives 14. Doubling 10 gives 20.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-628e8a2dd11bf626.mp3" },
    "7 × 2 = 14 and 10 × 2 = 20, so 7/10 = 14/20.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-d6ad35ac8fcc9320.mp3" },
    "Four to twenty-eight is times seven, so three to twenty-one must also use times seven.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-a168ffe80edbba36.mp3" },
    "Use the denominator to find the scale factor: four to twenty-eight.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-0ebec2b174b32144.mp3" },
    "Four times seven is twenty-eight, so three times seven is twenty-one.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-fe9b9f099b09574b.mp3" },
    "3 x 7 = 21 and 4 x 7 = 28, so 21/28 = 3/4.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-48b02966aaa3763d.mp3" },
    "4 became 28. Work out that multiplier first.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-89639fd188ad77c9.mp3" },
    "4 × 7 = 28, so apply ×7 to 3.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-b3ab52c3cc881392.mp3" },
    "3 × 7 = 21 and 4 × 7 = 28, so 21/28 = 3/4.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-88989110a5ea17a4.mp3" },
    "The bar is divided more finely, but the same two fifths is shaded: six of the fifteen sections.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-be2cf1145ddaa9b1.mp3" },
    "Five sections became fifteen. Find that multiplier first.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-703534cb8cfb88e7.mp3" },
    "That's times three. So the two filled parts also become times three: six filled sections.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-c49f1bf7349cf6e5.mp3" },
    "5 x 3 = 15, so 2 x 3 = 6. Six of the fifteen sections are filled.": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-becb336d32707c42.mp3" },
    "5 sections became 15 sections. What multiplier is that?": { src: "/fractions/audio/ryan-f5fd7f47a6917aba.mp3" },
